WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:09.280
In 2020, one singularity dominated the news. There&nbsp;
was one story that filled almost every front page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:16.160
of every paper, on every day. If I asked you&nbsp;
what was the biggest story from 2019, or 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.560
or 2017, or almost any other&nbsp;
year was, there’d be some debate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.680
but not in 2020. I don’t even have to tell&nbsp;
you what I’m talking about--you already know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:32.320
But just because the front pages were clogged up,&nbsp;
that’s not to say page two, three, or four were.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.360
While we may have stood still, the world&nbsp;
kept spinning, and news kept happening--some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.560
of it was heavy, some was sad, some was&nbsp;
bizarre, and some momentous--but what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.240
binds these stories together is that you likely&nbsp;
didn’t hear about them. By no fault of your own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.800
it was difficult to follow what was happening in&nbsp;
the world, because the only thing you could hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.560
was that one, singular story. The year’s not over&nbsp;
yet, though, at least as I’m saying this, and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.080
there’s still a chance to catch up on the news you&nbsp;
missed in 2020, from every country in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.840
Here’s how this’ll work. We ordered the countries&nbsp;
by population from largest to smallest according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:17.040
to 2019’s statistics. There are 195 of them:&nbsp;
193 countries recognized by the United Nations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.320
plus two UN-observer states: Palestine and&nbsp;
the Vatican City. This list was chosen because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.840
it is the most definitive list, it’s the list most&nbsp;
commonly used as a list of all countries, so some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.720
of its entries might include nations that you&nbsp;
or your government do not consider as countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.920
or omit ones that you or your government do&nbsp;
recognize. At the end of the day, we had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:44.640
pick a list, and if any is the list of countries,&nbsp;
it’s this one. Oh, and one more rule: the C word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.480
as in, the word that describes that one, singular&nbsp;
story of 2020, you’re not going to hear it once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.840
We start in China, the most populated country in&nbsp;
the world, which continues to expand its global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.960
presence, and in this case, its space presence&nbsp;
too. It became the second country in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.800
to plant a national flag on the moon, 50 years&nbsp;
after the United States did. China is trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.680
catch up with American and Russian planetary&nbsp;
endeavors, and, in recent years, has poured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.720
millions into its military-run space program&nbsp;
with the hopes of sending humans to the moon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.760
Another country with a burgeoning space-program&nbsp;
is India, where Amazon recently completed its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.560
largest office building in the world. The&nbsp;
1.8-million-square-foot behemoth was erected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:34.320
in Hyderabad--a city of nearly 10 million that’s&nbsp;
grown into a sizable technology and financial hub.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.400
India, as the world’s second-most-populous&nbsp;
country, is the fastest growing internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.880
market and Amazon joins other major tech&nbsp;
multinationals which have opened offices there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:46.720
though none with a campus that&nbsp;
spans 65 football fields.&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.960
A United States man that was facing&nbsp;
sentencing for two vehicle-theft charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.360
might be looking at a lot more jail time&nbsp;
now that he was caught faking his death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.440
His attorney submitted a fraudulent death&nbsp;
certificate, and it might have passed had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.000
there not been typos, like the misspelling&nbsp;
of “registry.” Stay in school, kids.&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.600
In Indonesia, a stimulus bill aimed at restarting&nbsp;
Southeast Asia’s largest economy was met with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.360
months of protests because citizens&nbsp;
said it slashed worker’s rights and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.720
environmental regulations. But, President&nbsp;
Joko Widodo signed the bill into law anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.360
saying it will bring over a 1 million jobs to&nbsp;
the struggling economy, which until this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.280
had been on an upward trajectory.&nbsp;
Opponents, though, argue it weakens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.840
rules that already don’t do enough to protect&nbsp;
the country’s natural resources and workers.&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:38.560
Rice is a serious business in southern Asia, and&nbsp;
it’s a profitable one too. That’s why Pakistan is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.960
vehemently fighting India over the&nbsp;
long grain—basmati to be specific.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.840
This year, India applied to the European Union&nbsp;
for a designation that recognizes basmati rice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.800
as an exclusive export of the country, even&nbsp;
though one-third of EU imports on the product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.720
come from Pakistan. In recent years, Pakistan’s&nbsp;
basmati rice exports to the EU have doubled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.480
while India’s are shrinking—which&nbsp;
makes the designation, if granted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:08.080
potentially more devastating and further&nbsp;
increases tensions between these two neighbors.&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.280
Between rain forest fires&nbsp;
and a negligent president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.480
the news streaming out of Brazil has been&nbsp;
heavy. But, a new discovery about the mating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:22.720
habits of the Thoropa taophora frog lightened&nbsp;
the feed. Frogs are either typically monogamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.480
or not—meaning they get around. This species&nbsp;
however, prefers “harem” style—one male to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.200
two or more specific females—which is much more&nbsp;
common in mammals throughout the animal kingdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:38.640
but not seen before in amphibians. Dubbed&nbsp;
“group fidelity,” finding this behavior in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.360
another set of four-legged animals checks&nbsp;
another box in the evolutionary list.&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:48.640
One particular virus has dominated the&nbsp;
news cycle this year, but in Nigeria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:53.600
they’re dealing with an entirely different&nbsp;
one too--the yellow fever. The disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:59.040
which is spread by mosquitoes, had basically&nbsp;
been eradicated for 20 years until 2017&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.960
when it crept back into the country because&nbsp;
of climate change and lower vaccination rates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.360
Now, it’s spiking. In the latter part of&nbsp;
2020, 75 people have already died and health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:14.480
officials worry that number will increase as&nbsp;
people are still hesitant to get vaccinated.&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.040
India is being beat in another metric as well,&nbsp;
and it’s an important one. Bangladesh most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:25.840
likely surpassed India in gross domestic product&nbsp;
this year, according to International Monetary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:31.120
Fund estimates. Why does that matter? Well,&nbsp;
India is the world’s sixth-largest economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:36.400
and its 10% GDP contraction is a&nbsp;
concerning indicator for the world economy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.760
Thanks to increased agricultural production and a&nbsp;
focus on developing its low-wage labor workforce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:47.200
Bangladesh, on the other hand, was able&nbsp;
to expand its GDP by 4% this year.&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.120
Another country that’s known to be slightly&nbsp;
competitive is Russia, and that’s a problem since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.560
they were banned from the biggest global sporting&nbsp;
competition of 2020 because of doping—that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:05.040
would be the Summer Olympics in Tokyo. Of course&nbsp;
the event was postponed until 2021 anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.200
but new investigations reveal that Russia&nbsp;
was planning to cyberattack the games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.640
which is something they’ve done before&nbsp;
when they attempted to wreak havoc on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.120
Winter Olympics in South Korea by dismantling&nbsp;
ticketing and wifi for the opening ceremony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.960
as well as targeting individual&nbsp;
broadcasters, sponsors, and athletes.&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:25.680
Before flights were ground to a halt in the&nbsp;
spring, one health insurer in Utah was taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.720
advantage of Mexico’s inexpensive pharmaceutical&nbsp;
prices and actually flying public employees from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.760
the United States to south of the border so they&nbsp;
could buy drugs at a fraction of American prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.480
They also got the benefit of those in-flight&nbsp;
snack peanuts and a free soda. It’s already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:44.560
saved the company a quarter of a million&nbsp;
dollars, and now other states are eyeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:49.360
the move—and may mimic it once flight schedules&nbsp;
resume to normal levels—meaning Mexico is bound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:54.640
to see an even greater boost to its already&nbsp;
burgeoning pharmaceutical tourism industry.&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.920
Fugaku is not just fun to say, it’s also&nbsp;
the name of the world’s speediest computer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:05.360
which was built this year in Japan. The&nbsp;
room-sized computer took first place in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:10.880
speed-ranking competition between supercomputers,&nbsp;
carrying out 2.8 times more calculations than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.920
second-place IBM machine built in the United&nbsp;
States. This is notable for the Japanese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:19.680
who have remained less competitive in&nbsp;
the supercomputer realm, often following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:25.360
the leads of the US and China. The systems are&nbsp;
used for complex military and scientific tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.080
including breaking code and modeling climate&nbsp;
change, and, of course, winning competitions.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:37.040
Africa is getting its own great wall: a green one.&nbsp;
The ambitious reforestation project is supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:43.600
to stretch 4,350 miles or 7,000 kilometers from&nbsp;
Djibouti to Senegal, with an aimed completion date&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:51.280
of 2030. Ethiopia is on track to meet its goal,&nbsp;
having already planted 5.5 billion seedlings. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:56.880
its neighbors are slow to start, and the project&nbsp;
is only 4% complete at the halfway mark, mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:02.560
due to financial struggles. It turns out, it takes&nbsp;
a lot of green to make a green wall, and so it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:07.600
proving difficult for some of the world’s poorest&nbsp;
countries to take this project to completion.&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.880
Money problems are also the reason that the&nbsp;
Philippines’ largest news network cited when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:18.080
shuttered dozens of its regional stations around&nbsp;
the country, but the audience isn’t buying this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:24.240
excuse. Instead, they’re wondering if it has more&nbsp;
to do with President Rodrigo Duterte’s criticism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:29.440
of the network’s coverage of his antidrug&nbsp;
platform. Either way, the loss will be felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:35.200
by millions of rural residents who rely on the&nbsp;
network as a lifeline for critical information.&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.920
Wealthy residents of Egypt are getting their&nbsp;
15 minutes of fame, just 2,500 years too late.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:47.280
More than 100 ornately painted wooden coffins&nbsp;
were unearthed by archaeologists near Saqqara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:53.120
20 miles or 30 kilometers south of Cairo. This&nbsp;
area is a large burial ground and is home to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:58.640
more than a dozen sites with similar tombs. This&nbsp;
discovery, however, is one of the most exciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:03.120
in recent years and comes at a time when the&nbsp;
country continues to try to draw tourists back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.800
after a sharp decline resulting from&nbsp;
the Arab Spring in the early 2010s.&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.760
Tourism isn’t a problem in Vietnam,&nbsp;
where 18 million people visit per year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:18.080
and that should mean liquor sales aren’t either.&nbsp;
But, a new zero-tolerance drunk-driving law that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:23.760
went into effect this year has taken the fizz out&nbsp;
of one of the world's faster growing beer markets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:29.600
Since implementation, beer sales have dropped&nbsp;
25% in the country, and violators face a fine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:36.480
of up to $345, which is double what the average&nbsp;
Vietnamese makes in a month. Even those who think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:42.400
riding a bike is a safe alternative will be&nbsp;
shocked with a $25 fine for drunk-pedaling.&nbsp;

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.440
In other 2020 epidemic news, the&nbsp;
World Health Organization declared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:52.000
that an ebola outbreak in the Democratic&nbsp;
Republic of the Congo was officially over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:59.040
after two years and 2,280 deaths. It was&nbsp;
the country’s 10th outbreak of the disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:02.800
but its second most deadly, stemming&nbsp;
from people’s mistrust of the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:07.280
and feuding between officials. That doesn’t&nbsp;
mean the country is in the clear though, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:12.320
it’s now dealing with the world’s largest measles&nbsp;
epidemic, and, of course, the global pandemic.&nbsp;

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:19.520
Germans are taking a stand, and, well, technically&nbsp;
a sit, in Dannenröder forest, south of Frankfurt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:23.120
where they’ve been camped out to oppose the&nbsp;
government’s construction of a highway that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:30.240
will route through the wild lands and cut down 67&nbsp;
acres or 27 hectares of 250-year-old trees. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.640
activists are calling the government hypocritical&nbsp;
for cutting down cherished trees when it should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:40.080
focus on public transportation infrastructure, and&nbsp;
both sides expect standoffs for months to come.&nbsp;

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:46.320
Also bad news for trees are the more than 9&nbsp;
million that have died in Turkey. Last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:52.080
as part of National Forestation Day, volunteers&nbsp;
across the country planted 11 million trees across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:59.280
more than 2,000 sites—including 303,150 saplings&nbsp;
planted in Çorum, which broke the world record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:05.040
for most trees planted in a single location. Now,&nbsp;
officials are reporting that up to 90% of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:09.680
have died because they either weren’t planted&nbsp;
properly or lacked enough rainfall to grow.&nbsp;

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:14.240
In neighboring Iran, a woman was filmed&nbsp;
riding a bike without a hijab near a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:20.400
major mosque in Najafabad. Why is this news?&nbsp;
Well, in Iran, it’s been against Islamic law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:26.480
since 1979 for women to be seen in public without&nbsp;
a covering for their head and hair and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:31.680
largely obeyed. Though women have started to&nbsp;
push the limits in larger cities, like Tehran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:35.200
it’s still widely criticized&nbsp;
and, in this case, punishable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:39.840
The woman was arrested for this display,&nbsp;
which was widely shared on social media.&nbsp;

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:46.240
Another arrestable offense, this time in&nbsp;
Thailand, is writing a bad online review--yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:50.640
you heard that correctly. An American living&nbsp;
in the Southeast Asian country wasn’t happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:55.360
with being charged a $15 corkage fee at a&nbsp;
beach resort in Koh Chang, so he did what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.360 --> 00:12:00.400
any thumb-happy traveler would do and wrote&nbsp;
a negative TripAdvisor review. The hotel was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:05.120
equally displeased and had him arrested on&nbsp;
defamation charges, which are notably harsh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:10.720
in a country that attempts to stifle some free&nbsp;
speech. He therefore spent a weekend in jail.&nbsp;

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:16.640
In more turbulent waters, construction started on&nbsp;
what will soon be the world’s largest off-shore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:21.200
wind farm. When completed, the Dogger Wind&nbsp;
Farm in the North Sea off the eastern coast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:26.720
of the United Kingdom, will provide enough&nbsp;
energy to power 4.5 million homes every year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:32.080
or 5% of England’s total energy demand.&nbsp;
The enormous energy production capability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:36.080
is made possible by their use of one&nbsp;
of the world’s largest wind turbines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:44.240
the Haliade-X, which stands 853 feet or 260&nbsp;
meters tall and has 351-foot or 107-meter blades.&nbsp;

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:49.760
Requiring much less power than that, though, is&nbsp;
the Ami, a mini car released in France that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:56.000
be driven without a license by anyone over 14.&nbsp;
Manufactured by Citroen, the all-electric Ami is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:02.960
what’s officially dubbed a light quadricycle. The&nbsp;
four-wheel cube can travel 28 mph or 45 km/h with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:08.960
a range of 46 miles or 75 kilometers, and it’s got&nbsp;
a mini price too, ringing in at just over $7,000.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:15.760
Another European product is making headlines,&nbsp;
but this time it’s for lack of it. The world’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:21.280
second-largest producer of olive oil, Italy, has&nbsp;
been plagued by bad weather and a deadly bacteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:26.960
cutting production by more than a quarter this&nbsp;
year. The Xylella bacteria, also called leprosy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:31.280
has hit the olive trees in the Puglia region&nbsp;
particularly hard, and those effects will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:36.400
felt globally, especially in the United States,&nbsp;
which purchases one third of Italy’s oil.&nbsp;

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:43.760
At the southern end of the world, a 116-year-old&nbsp;
man died in South Africa. He was, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:47.360
the oldest man in the world at the time&nbsp;
of his passing, at least unofficially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:51.920
having lived through the Spanish Flu,&nbsp;
which took the lives of his entire family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:57.760
He credited his long life to “God’s grace,” but&nbsp;
it may have been health and strength too—a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:03.520
weeks before he died of natural causes, his family&nbsp;
said he was chopping wood with a 4-pound hammer.&nbsp;

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:08.240
Up the African coast, the two largest&nbsp;
Tanzanite gemstones ever unearthed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:12.080
were pulled out of the only country&nbsp;
in which they’re found: Tanzania.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:18.640
The two dark violet-blue minerals, each about 11&nbsp;
inches or 30 cm long and 4 inches or 10 cm thick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:24.160
rang in roughly $3.35 million for the mining&nbsp;
boss who runs the 200-person operation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:28.640
the country’s northern region. He did what&nbsp;
anybody who struck the motherlode would do:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:33.120
promised to build a school and shopping&nbsp;
mall, but first threw a neighborhood party.&nbsp;

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:38.160
Multiple drug raids in the hills of Myanmar&nbsp;
revealed a complex and burgeoning synthetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:42.640
drug market that’s speculated to be fueling&nbsp;
global demand for opioids like methamphetamine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:46.880
and fentanyl. The seizures were the&nbsp;
largest finds in Southeast Asian history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:51.120
and represent a transition away from China,&nbsp;
which had previously been supplying many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:55.040
of the chemicals for synthetics but&nbsp;
cracked down under worldwide pressure.

00:14:55.840 --> 00:15:02.320
In Kenya, the score is 1 for the fig tree, 0 for&nbsp;
developers. A four-story tree in the country’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.320 --> 00:15:07.280
capital of Nairobi was set to be cut down to make&nbsp;
way for an expressway but environmentalists put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:13.200
up a fight—and they won. Not only will the tree be&nbsp;
preserved in its spot, but it’s now been elevated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:18.960
to legend status, gaining notoriety both as an&nbsp;
environmental symbol and as a call to action to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:24.400
protect even more green space in a city that’s&nbsp;
constantly seeing parks and nature eliminated.&nbsp;

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:28.640
In grimmer news, the mayor of&nbsp;
Seoul, the capital of South Korea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:33.680
was found dead in a park this summer. He&nbsp;
was a potential presidential candidate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:36.000
the second-most powerful person in the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:41.760
so the passing sent unrest through the nation.&nbsp;
Several days before his death, a former secretary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:46.000
accused him of sexual harassment—especially&nbsp;
shocking since he was a champion of women’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:50.240
rights in a male-dominated country—so&nbsp;
his death is being treated as a suicide.&nbsp;

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:55.680
A less grim story comes from another mayor on&nbsp;
the other side of the world in Bogota, Columbia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.680 --> 00:16:01.040
who recently became the first openly lesbian&nbsp;
mayor ever for the capital city in January.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:04.480
This was an historic moment for&nbsp;
the conservative Catholic country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:09.280
and Latin America in general. Claudia&nbsp;
Lopez married her partner in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:14.880
and is recognized for being a progressive beacon&nbsp;
in a region that’s slow to progress LGBTQ rights.&nbsp;

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:19.440
Yachting in the Iberian Sea was a little&nbsp;
more raucous than usual this fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:22.720
after a string of orca incidents&nbsp;
were reported in Spain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:28.080
in which killer whales were targeting boat rudders&nbsp;
and causing damage to more than a dozen vessels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:33.440
The increasingly aggressive interactions initially&nbsp;
puzzled researchers, but they now believe the trio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:38.480
of orcas—which accounted for almost two-thirds&nbsp;
of the two dozen reports—were previously injured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:43.360
by a boat and perhaps reacting defensively to&nbsp;
yachts because of those traumatic incidents.&nbsp;

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:48.080
Another boating story brings us to&nbsp;
Argentina, where a determined sailor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:53.360
finally docked on land after completing an&nbsp;
85-day journey across the Atlantic Ocean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:57.760
The Portugeuse resident set sail in mid-March&nbsp;
after lockdown restrictions kept him from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:02.320
traveling to see his 90-year-old father. He&nbsp;
would not be deterred by the lack of flights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:08.240
and decided to voyage by sea in his 29-foot boat,&nbsp;
accompanied by rice, canned tuna, and fruit.&nbsp;

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:14.160
Uganda is home to 50% of the world’s mountain&nbsp;
gorillas, so when one of the country’s most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:18.800
famous, Rafiki, was killed by a poacher,&nbsp;
the government took a harder stance than it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:24.960
had previously. Officials chose to jail the&nbsp;
killer for six years. This landmark ruling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:29.840
sends a message to future poachers that animal&nbsp;
killings won’t be tolerated and is a win for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:33.920
wildlife groups which have been calling for&nbsp;
more restrictive measures for many years.&nbsp;

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:41.280
In terms of on-brand news for 2020, wildfires&nbsp;
coming within 1 mile of Chernobyl in Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:46.320
has to be near the top of the list. An 18-mile&nbsp;
radius exclusion zone was set up after the nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:51.280
disaster there in the ‘80s, and this wildfire&nbsp;
spread into that area, causing officials to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:56.400
worry that not only would radioactive material be&nbsp;
dispersed into the air, but that it might threaten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:02.560
existing structures and a disposal site. More than&nbsp;
300 firefighters were able to control the burn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:06.320
preventing this lesser-known story&nbsp;
from becoming a major headline.&nbsp;

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:11.120
When a migrant rescue boat came across a&nbsp;
backpack floating in the Meditteranean Sea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:15.040
officials assumed its owners had perished&nbsp;
in crossing from Algeria to Italy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:19.680
as is often the case with many found personal&nbsp;
objects floating in this part of the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:24.160
But this time, there was a happy ending:&nbsp;
An NGO in Italy was able to track down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:27.920
the owners of the personal items, who&nbsp;
were migrants that survived a shipwreck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:32.080
and return the contents to them, which&nbsp;
included their inscribed wedding rings.&nbsp;

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:37.840
Sudan is taking baby steps toward democracy,&nbsp;
after three decades of oppressive laws that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:42.480
were primarily targeted toward women. Of&nbsp;
highest priority is banning female genital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:47.680
mutilation--a typically religious practice&nbsp;
that affects almost 90% of Sudanese women.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:51.680
The new transitional government is also&nbsp;
loosening wardrobe restrictions for women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:55.840
and saying OK to alcohol consumption, at&nbsp;
least for non-Muslims in the country.&nbsp;

00:18:56.800 --> 00:19:02.640
The 3,600-year-old Gilgamesh Dream Tablet is being&nbsp;
returned to its rightful home in what is now Iraq,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:07.280
where it originated, after a curious and illegal&nbsp;
journey that brought it to the Museum of the Bible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:12.160
in Washington DC, which was co-founded by none&nbsp;
other than tablet king and crafts retail giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:17.360
Hobby Lobby. The rare cuneiform tablet features&nbsp;
one of the oldest works of literature in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:24.240
world, the Epic of Gilgamesh, and was purchased by&nbsp;
Hobby Lobby at auction for $1.6 million in 2014.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:28.880
This isn’t the first time Hobby Lobby has had&nbsp;
problems with the provenance of its acquisitions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:34.400
as it previously had to forfeit 5,500&nbsp;
artifacts and pay a $3 million fine.&nbsp;

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:39.840
National identity cards in Afghanistan&nbsp;
include a person’s name, date of birth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:45.280
and now, both of their parents’ names,&nbsp;
which sounds pretty normal, but previously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:50.160
just the father’s name was listed because using&nbsp;
a woman’s in public was traditionally frowned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:55.040
upon and even considered an insult—it’s a&nbsp;
restriction that goes so far that much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.040 --> 00:20:00.880
Afghanistan does not list females’ names on their&nbsp;
gravestones. This step in updating ID cards is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:05.840
seen as a small victory for women’s rights&nbsp;
in the overwhelmingly conservative country.&nbsp;

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:12.000
When a herd of elephants at Poland’s Warsaw zoo&nbsp;
became sad after losing their matriarch—which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:16.560
lead a given herd—veterinarians turned&nbsp;
to Colorado’s favorite stress reliever:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:21.360
medical marijuana. The three African&nbsp;
elephants are being given doses of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:25.120
liquid cannabis through their trunks, and&nbsp;
researchers will study hormone levels in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:29.200
their blood over the next two years to&nbsp;
monitor how it affects their stress.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:35.440
A small town of 7,000 people in Canada gave&nbsp;
itself a new identity when they voted this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:40.480
year to change the city’s name from Asbestos&nbsp;
to Val-des-Sources, or Valley of the Springs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:44.880
The town got its name for being home to&nbsp;
the largest asbestos mine in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:48.960
but since the mineral has been linked&nbsp;
to causing cancer and the mine has shut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:55.040
residents voted to disassociate from the toxic&nbsp;
substance. But it wasn’t without a fight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:58.880
More veteran members of the community were&nbsp;
opposed to the name change as they felt it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:04.400
was unnecessary, because in French, which is&nbsp;
spoken there, the word for asbestos is amiante.&nbsp;

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:10.240
Tensions are simmering in a United Nations-backed&nbsp;
buffer zone between Morocco and Mauritania,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:15.840
which could bring a 29-year truce to a&nbsp;
boiling point. The Western Sahara-contested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:21.120
zone has long been controlled by Morocco under&nbsp;
a ceasefire agreement with the Polisario Front,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:26.320
a pro-independence movement. But now, the&nbsp;
front has blocked transportation between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:30.720
two countries, and Morocco launched&nbsp;
a military operation in response.&nbsp;

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:33.840
On the same latitude, but a few time zones over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:38.160
Saudi Arabia wants to maintain its claim&nbsp;
as the world’s top energy exporter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:44.640
but now with hydrogen. Blue hydrogen is a carbon&nbsp;
dioxide byproduct of reforming natural gas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:50.000
and Saudi Arabia sent its first cargo shipment&nbsp;
of it to Japan this year, all in an effort to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:55.120
profit off of the world’s transition to greener&nbsp;
fuels. Part of this goal also includes plans to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.120 --> 00:22:01.040
build a $5 billion hydrogen-based ammonia plant&nbsp;
powered by renewable energy on the Red Sea.

00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:06.960
Uzbekistan’s language can be written in&nbsp;
three ways: Arabic, Cyrillic, and Latin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.960 --> 00:22:11.520
but it’s the latter that will soon be the norm,&nbsp;
thanks to a decree by the country’s president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:16.560
which called for a faster transition away from&nbsp;
Cyrillic. Though Uzbek was originally written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:22.160
in Arabic, Russian influence during the early&nbsp;
20th century changed it to Cyrillic and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:27.520
to distance itself from Russia, Uzbekistan wants&nbsp;
to move toward Latin script, consisting of 29&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:32.880
letters and 1 apostrophe, to help develop&nbsp;
a stronger, more unique national identity.&nbsp;

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:39.280
Peruvians are hoping that the third time’s a charm&nbsp;
after three presidents reigned in just one week.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:44.400
Francisco Sagasti will now lead the country,&nbsp;
following a Congress-led ousting of one president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:50.000
and a five-day term-turned-resignation by the&nbsp;
second. Sagasti is seen as a consensus-builder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:54.640
so citizens are hopeful, but the tumultuous&nbsp;
leadership changes over the past several years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.640 --> 00:22:59.440
leave a lot of uncertainty, and,&nbsp;
frankly, apathy, at this point.&nbsp;

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:01.920
If there’s a baby boom this spring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:09.120
a shortage of condom manufacturing in March may&nbsp;
be the culprit. A Malaysian company, Karex Bhd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:14.320
makes one in every five condoms globally, and&nbsp;
when the pandemic hit this year, facilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:20.320
were forced to shut down resulting in at least&nbsp;
100 million fewer condoms worldwide. It ramped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:26.560
back up quickly, but the effects of a shortage&nbsp;
like this could be at least nine months away.&nbsp;

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:32.640
It’s been a year for Africa’s richest woman,&nbsp;
Isabel dos Santos. She’s the eldest daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:37.200
of Angola’s former longtime leader Jose&nbsp;
Eduardo dos Santos, and in January was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:42.160
charged with embezzlement and laundering&nbsp;
more than $1 billion from her native country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:48.560
Then, in October, her husband died in, reportedly,&nbsp;
a diving accident off the coast of Dubai. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:52.800
an avid art collector, and also tied up&nbsp;
in the scandal she faced, meaning it’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:57.920
tragic tale with lots of plot twists, that&nbsp;
doesn’t seem to have a final chapter yet.&nbsp;

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:04.320
Another story about $1 billion revolves around&nbsp;
the United Kingdom’s investment in Mozambique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:09.120
This year, a deal was inked to send UK&nbsp;
taxpayer-funded financial support to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:14.000
and export the African country’s gas reserves—one&nbsp;
of the largest financing projects on the continent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:18.640
ever, according to some reports—but citizens and&nbsp;
environmental groups are shouting “hypocrite”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:23.040
as the UK continues to claim it’s a global&nbsp;
leader in the fight against climate change.&nbsp;

00:24:23.600 --> 00:24:29.760
Speaking of oil in Africa, a floating storage and&nbsp;
offloading facility moored off the coast of Yemen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:34.080
continues to deteriorate and threatens&nbsp;
to spill 1.1 million barrels of crude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:39.520
oil into the Red Sea, which would be four&nbsp;
times the amount of the Exxon Valdez spill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:44.960
The war in Yemen has prevented the United Nations&nbsp;
from doing anything to prevent a potential leak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:49.520
but recently Houthi rebels—which control the&nbsp;
area where the vessel is stuck and the oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:54.960
company that owns it—gave permission for world&nbsp;
leaders to move ahead with rehabilitation plans.&nbsp;

00:24:55.760 --> 00:25:00.960
The African country of Ghana may be a respite&nbsp;
for Black Americans seeking refuge from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:05.680
United States’ ongoing racial injustice, and&nbsp;
Ghanian officials are actually recruiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:11.040
members of the African diaspora. The government&nbsp;
set up a handful of enticements for transplants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.040 --> 00:25:16.000
including land deals, expatriate guides, and&nbsp;
quicker paths to citizenship, and all of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:21.680
comes on the heels of a 45% increase in visitor&nbsp;
numbers from the United States last year.&nbsp;

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:27.280
Math, science, and reading are, of course,&nbsp;
required subjects for children in school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.280 --> 00:25:34.800
and now, in one country, downward dog is too. In&nbsp;
March, Nepal became the first nation in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:40.880
to incorporate yoga into its mandatory curriculum.&nbsp;
Students in the Himalayan country won’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.880 --> 00:25:46.080
practice the poses, but they’ll learn about the&nbsp;
history of yogic thought and Ayurvedic medicine.&nbsp;

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:52.320
Children, or the idea of them at least, are&nbsp;
making headlines in Venezuela, where controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:58.080
Twitter-happy President Nicolas Maduro asked for&nbsp;
every woman in the country to have six children.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.080 --> 00:26:03.520
The plea was met with immediate backlash because&nbsp;
the country is in a deep economic recession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:07.680
and is unable to provide essential services&nbsp;
like food and healthcare for the millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:13.440
of residents it already has. He, apparently,&nbsp;
views more children as part of the solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:16.640
though he offered little in the&nbsp;
way of specifics as to how.&nbsp;

00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:23.280
Ever heard of Voeltzkow’s chameleon? No? Well,&nbsp;
that’s OK, almost nobody has, since the elusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:29.200
reptile hadn’t been seen in almost 100 years and&nbsp;
was actually placed on the extinction list. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.200 --> 00:26:34.400
this year a team of researchers spotted it&nbsp;
on the northwest tip of Madagascar. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.400 --> 00:26:37.040
the reptile is only thought to&nbsp;
live during the rainy season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.040 --> 00:26:42.240
making it a “mayfly of vertebrae.” This, of&nbsp;
course, makes it even more vulnerable to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:47.040
deforestation that continues to threaten&nbsp;
their sheer existence, but its rediscovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:51.040
gives scientists hope for other species&nbsp;
which were also thought to be extinct.&nbsp;

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:55.120
More than 40% of North Korean men smoke, but a new&nbsp;
law that bans puffing down in public places might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:55.120 --> 00:28:01.040
smolder that statistic. Citing health reasons,&nbsp;
tobacco is now prohibited in specific venues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:01.040 --> 00:28:05.920
such as political and ideological education&nbsp;
centers, theaters, cinemas, and medical and public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:11.200
health facilities. One person who might struggle&nbsp;
with this new law: Leader Kim Jong Un, who is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:11.200 --> 00:28:16.480
chain smoker and frequently photographed with&nbsp;
a cigarette in hand. But, the country is moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:21.288
forward with the prohibition despite being unable&nbsp;
to convince its supreme leader to kick the habit.&nbsp;

00:28:21.288 --> 00:28:21.371
Political unrest looms over the Ivory Coast&nbsp;
after President Alassane Ouattara won,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.371 --> 00:28:21.468
controversially, a third term in November. When&nbsp;
his planned successor suddenly died this summer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.468 --> 00:28:21.516
Ouattara reneged on his pledge to leave office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.516 --> 00:28:21.593
and even though he received 94% of the&nbsp;
vote, many, including the opposition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.593 --> 00:28:21.686
saw the process as unfair. It’s indicative of&nbsp;
a wider trend in African politics, which has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.686 --> 00:28:21.766
seen third-term bids and constitutional&nbsp;
amendments increasing in recent years.&nbsp;

00:28:21.766 --> 00:28:21.860
The unearthing of four 8,000-year-old skeletons&nbsp;
in Cameroon is a groundbreaking find that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:21.959
providing new information about our current human&nbsp;
species. The DNA from these skeletons offers the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.959 --> 00:28:22.056
first genetic material from West African humans,&nbsp;
painting a better picture of how ancient people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.056 --> 00:28:22.149
split into four genetic groups, both physically&nbsp;
and geographically. Of the four populations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.149 --> 00:28:22.240
it’s been the West African genes that have&nbsp;
remained elusive to researchers—until now.&nbsp;

00:28:22.240 --> 00:28:26.800
Another big find was recently made by scientists,&nbsp;
this time in the Great Barrier Reef off the coast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:26.800 --> 00:28:32.400
of Australia, where a pinnacle of coral one third&nbsp;
of a mile long was discovered by researchers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:32.400 --> 00:28:36.560
While you might think that the world-famous&nbsp;
natural wonder would be thoroughly mapped out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.560 --> 00:28:42.960
this was actually the first major new element&nbsp;
of the reef to be discovered in over 120 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:47.360
Of course, the reef is shrinking by the year&nbsp;
due to the effects of climate-change and coral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.360 --> 00:28:52.240
bleaching, so even the discovery of this&nbsp;
comparatively small section is great news.&nbsp;

00:28:53.040 --> 00:28:57.120
Niger is one of the world’s poorest&nbsp;
countries, but its location also makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:57.120 --> 00:29:02.240
it a key regional recipient of western aid&nbsp;
funding the fight against Islamic terrorism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:08.560
That’s why an audit of its defense spending that&nbsp;
revealed a loss of $137 million over eight years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.560 --> 00:29:14.160
raised more than eyebrows. Apparently the country&nbsp;
was overcharged in several instances—sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:18.560
by nearly $20 million—for important&nbsp;
military equipment, and western countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:23.200
that grant Niger money are questioning their&nbsp;
decisions, and potentially future giving.&nbsp;

00:29:23.920 --> 00:29:28.240
You don’t have to worry about the spread of&nbsp;
airborne viruses in Sri Lanka’s newest museum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.240 --> 00:29:32.960
because it’s... underwater. Created by naval&nbsp;
officers off the southern tip of the island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:36.480
nation, the country’s first-ever&nbsp;
below-water museum is a collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:41.200
of artifacts and sculptures installed 50&nbsp;
feet beneath the surface. The attraction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:41.200 --> 00:29:46.720
is not only intended to draw tourists, but it&nbsp;
should lure fish and spur coral growth too.

00:29:47.280 --> 00:29:52.560
One of Burkina Faso’s largest gold mines was&nbsp;
shut down for almost a year after 39 employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:57.120
were killed in an attack on their convoy.&nbsp;
Since then, the Canadian-owned Boungou mine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.120 --> 00:30:02.000
has restarted with tightened security measures and&nbsp;
improved infrastructure. During the year hiatus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:07.040
the mine’s owners actually changed from one&nbsp;
Canadian company to another for $735 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:12.640
which is notable because, even though agriculture&nbsp;
still accounts for 80% of the country’s economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.640 --> 00:30:17.920
gold exports driven by foreign investments&nbsp;
continue to develop alternative economic sectors.&nbsp;

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:22.720
And on the subject of West African unrest,&nbsp;
a military coup in Mali ousted the country’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:27.040
president and prime minister in August,&nbsp;
following eight years of disrupted elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:32.800
government corruption, and Islamist insurgency.&nbsp;
Military colonels remain in power today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.800 --> 00:30:37.120
This further instability could send ripple&nbsp;
effects across the region because of Mali’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:40.960
strategic location for Western countries&nbsp;
such as France and the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:40.960 --> 00:30:44.560
which have actively tried to prevent&nbsp;
increased tensions in the area.&nbsp;

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:51.280
File this story under stranger than fiction,&nbsp;
because, well, it’s about nonfiction. Some 200&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:56.480
books, including first editions by Galileo and&nbsp;
Sir Isaac Newton, were found buried underneath a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:56.480 --> 00:31:02.240
rural Romanian cottage, after a three-year search&nbsp;
that spanned 45 addresses and three countries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.240 --> 00:31:08.720
The $3.35 million worth of books were stolen&nbsp;
during a heist from a London warehouse in 2017.&nbsp;

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:12.080
Looking to get away from it&nbsp;
all? For a cool $20 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:12.720 --> 00:31:19.680
you can buy a 50,000-acre island off the coast of&nbsp;
Chile. Acquiring private islands is nothing new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.680 --> 00:31:22.720
but this one is making headlines&nbsp;
because environmentalists and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:27.440
indigenous population are not happy that&nbsp;
this biodiversity hotspot has a price tag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:27.440 --> 00:31:32.000
at all. Even though the island has been in&nbsp;
private hands for a century, they’re worried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:37.440
that new ownership could jeopardize its future&nbsp;
conservation and sets a dangerous precedent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:43.200
In an effort to bolster its economy, Malawi became&nbsp;
the fifth African country to decriminalize medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:48.000
marijuana. Almost 80% of the country’s&nbsp;
population is employed in agriculture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:53.200
and it used to be a major producer of tobacco,&nbsp;
but, as global demand for tobacco declines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:58.800
it’s looking for alternative revenue streams.&nbsp;
Enter, Malawi Gold, an already popular strain of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.800 --> 00:32:03.840
recreational cannabis. The country’s dry climate&nbsp;
is actually conducive to growing marijuana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:09.680
and as the herb’s popularity increases around the&nbsp;
world, Malawians plan to jump on the ganja train.&nbsp;

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:14.480
Kazakhstan is known for a few&nbsp;
things: its oil and gas economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:14.480 --> 00:32:18.400
its proximity to Russia and Mongolia,&nbsp;
and just generally for being a “stan,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:23.120
but in Western culture it’s perhaps most&nbsp;
famous for one of its fictional natives:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:28.240
Borat Sagdiyev. Played by actor Sacha Baron&nbsp;
Cohen, the satirical character Borat pokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:32.560
fun at Kazakhstan with the catchphrase “Very&nbsp;
nice!” but officials there realized those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:37.600
words have staying power, and recently officially&nbsp;
adopted them as their official tourism slogan.&nbsp;

00:32:38.240 --> 00:32:43.360
The African Ansell mole-rat, native to&nbsp;
Zambia, is near-blind, burrows underground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:48.160
and this year, scientists discovered it&nbsp;
can sense magnetic fields with its eyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.160 --> 00:32:52.640
Though the animal’s eyes are barely functional,&nbsp;
discerning between just light and dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.640 --> 00:32:57.200
this new discovery gives more clues as to how&nbsp;
the creature travels and lives in an underground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.200 --> 00:33:03.200
network up to 1.7 miles or 2.7 kilometers long,&nbsp;
and of course, it may help humans in our own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:03.200 --> 00:33:08.720
scientific advances, as we learn more about&nbsp;
nature, and as we often do, try to mimic it.&nbsp;

00:33:09.280 --> 00:33:14.640
Government officials in Guatemala passed a&nbsp;
$12.2 billion budget that raised stipends for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:19.520
representatives’ meals while cutting funding for&nbsp;
human rights agencies, healthcare, and education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:23.280
triggering residents to protest in the&nbsp;
streets and set the congressional building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:28.720
on fire. Their anger at the misplaced spending&nbsp;
comes on the heels of not only the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:32.560
but two destructive hurricanes that&nbsp;
tore through the country this fall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:36.400
It turns out, tragedy after tragedy&nbsp;
is when people need help the most.&nbsp;

00:33:37.120 --> 00:33:41.040
In July, Ecuador reported that more&nbsp;
than 300 Chinese fishing vessels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:44.400
were encroaching on its exclusive&nbsp;
fishing zones near the Galapagos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:44.400 --> 00:33:49.760
with the illegal fleets going as far as to turn&nbsp;
off their tracking devices to remain undetected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.760 --> 00:33:54.480
So the South American country joined coastal&nbsp;
neighbors Chile, Peru, and Colombia to file&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.480 --> 00:33:58.640
a joint statement—or rather, a light&nbsp;
threat—about protecting their waters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:58.640 --> 00:34:03.360
from the increasing creep of foreign fishing,&nbsp;
and ultimately, China’s expanding presence.&nbsp;

00:34:04.080 --> 00:34:09.200
Overseas, some new research into the life of the&nbsp;
Netherlands’ most famous artist, Vincent Van Gogh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.200 --> 00:34:14.160
reveals that he didn’t suffer from schizophrenia&nbsp;
or syphilis, as some have theorized, but rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:14.160 --> 00:34:19.360
that the delirium leading up to his death may be&nbsp;
attributed to alcohol withdrawal. The artist was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:25.040
institutionalized twice—once after cutting off&nbsp;
his ear—and reported hallucinations both times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:25.040 --> 00:34:28.960
Researchers now believe these symptoms&nbsp;
were due to his lack of wine and absinthe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:28.960 --> 00:34:33.840
which he consumed in great amounts during the&nbsp;
most prolific, and final, years of his life.&nbsp;

00:34:34.640 --> 00:34:41.200
In somber news, Syria topped an unenviable&nbsp;
list in 2019, being named the deadliest place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.200 --> 00:34:45.840
in the world for humanitarian workers. The&nbsp;
report on the subject, released this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:50.960
details that the number of people killed helping&nbsp;
the war-torn country rose sharply last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:50.960 --> 00:34:56.000
with the majority of deaths stemming from aerial&nbsp;
strikes. Officials say the grim statistic will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:56.000 --> 00:35:00.320
make it harder to recruit aid workers to&nbsp;
a place where they’re needed the most.

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:06.640
One rodent that’s turning its species’ reputation&nbsp;
around is Magawa, a land-mine-sniffing rat in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:12.960
Cambodia. The 5-year-old African pouched rat has&nbsp;
discovered 39 land mines, 28 pieces of unexploded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:18.240
ordnance, and helped clear more than 1.5 million&nbsp;
square feet of land over the past four years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:23.520
rightfully earning itself the gold medal from&nbsp;
the People’s Dispensary for Sick Animals. That,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:28.640
unsurprisingly, makes it the first rat to receive&nbsp;
an award that is usually reserved for humans.&nbsp;

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:35.600
After the tragic port explosion in Beirut, Senegal&nbsp;
moved quickly to avoid a similar catastrophe and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:42.000
removed 3,050 tons of ammonium nitrate from its&nbsp;
port. The substance was transported to Mali, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:47.360
it was used to make bombs for mining operations.&nbsp;
Ammonium nitrate is commonly found in fertilizers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:52.240
and explosives, so ports around the world&nbsp;
which temporarily house the volatile compound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:57.120
such as Senegal’s Dakar, made sure they weren’t&nbsp;
in the same vulnerable situation as Beirut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:57.120 --> 00:36:01.520
where the substance led to the accidental&nbsp;
explosion that killed 190 people.&nbsp;

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:06.240
South African extractive economies&nbsp;
continue to make headlines, such as Chad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.240 --> 00:36:10.880
which recently asked UNESCO to postpone&nbsp;
the process for designating Lake Chad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:10.880 --> 00:36:17.120
as a World Heritage Site. Why? Because Chad&nbsp;
wants to explore oil and gas development in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:22.640
the lake area—which directly opposes UNESCO’s&nbsp;
conservation goals for World Heritage Sites.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:22.640 --> 00:36:28.320
It also came as a surprise to Cameroon, Niger,&nbsp;
and Nigeria, which cooperated on the two-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:32.640
application process for the multinational&nbsp;
lake to gain this distinctive designation.&nbsp;

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:38.160
More fish theft brings us to the coast of&nbsp;
Somalia, which stretches 2,000 miles or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.160 --> 00:36:43.840
3,200 kilometers and is the longest coastline&nbsp;
in Africa. That expanse makes it harder for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:48.880
the country to police its waters, where&nbsp;
poachers like a 192-vessel fleet from Iran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.880 --> 00:36:54.640
are illegally fishing in the country’s exclusive&nbsp;
economic zone. The size of the Iranian operation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:54.640 --> 00:36:59.600
discovered this year, is a blow to Somalia,&nbsp;
where one in three residents face hunger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:59.600 --> 00:37:04.320
and it demonstrates the rising tensions&nbsp;
over the planet’s limited natural resources.

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:08.880
One resource being exported out of Africa&nbsp;
that benefits from increased demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:14.400
is a Zimbabwean film that became the country’s&nbsp;
first feature to make it to Netflix. The movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.400 --> 00:37:19.120
“Cook Off,” is about a woman who enters a TV&nbsp;
cooking show, and it was made for just $8,000—an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:26.400
impressive feat given that it was produced in&nbsp;
2017 just before the country’s economy collapsed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:26.400 --> 00:37:30.880
This acquisition by the streaming giant gives&nbsp;
more credibility to Zimbabwe’s film industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.880 --> 00:37:35.840
and means that the production crew&nbsp;
and actors may finally get paid.&nbsp;

00:37:35.840 --> 00:37:40.240
A small band of chimpanzees in Guinea that&nbsp;
are known for their remarkable use of tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:45.600
were thought to be near extinction until the&nbsp;
tribe’s last fertile female gave birth to a beacon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:45.600 --> 00:37:51.600
of hope: another female. Deforestation and&nbsp;
isolation have led to the tribe’s demise—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:51.600 --> 00:37:55.840
this group is of particular interest to&nbsp;
researchers because they mimic human’s use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.840 --> 00:38:01.040
of tools. But this bundle of joy has also created&nbsp;
momentum from the country’s residents to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.040 --> 00:38:05.520
protections and forestation to ensure&nbsp;
there is a future for the chimpanzees.&nbsp;

00:38:06.240 --> 00:38:10.320
One of the world’s most wanted fugitives&nbsp;
was found this year in a small apartment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.320 --> 00:38:15.760
on the outskirts of Paris. Félicien Kabuga was&nbsp;
arrested for his role in the Rwandan genocide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:15.760 --> 00:38:20.640
which killed 800,000 members of the&nbsp;
Tutsi ethnic minority, among others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:20.640 --> 00:38:26.960
in 1994. Kabuga will be tried in a UN tribunal&nbsp;
court next year, and this capture renews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:32.400
efforts to find others on the run who played&nbsp;
significant roles in the country’s darkest days.&nbsp;

00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:38.080
France and Benin also made the news in 2020,&nbsp;
with the former’s government fast-tracking a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.080 --> 00:38:43.120
bill aimed at returning looted objects to the&nbsp;
Sub-Saharan country within a year. The move is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.120 --> 00:38:47.520
part of a multi-year restitution effort between&nbsp;
European countries and their former colonies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:51.760
and this law specifically returns 26 royal&nbsp;
artifacts that were stolen from the Abomey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.760 --> 00:38:56.720
Palace during the 19th century. These&nbsp;
cultural repatriation efforts are ongoing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.720 --> 00:39:02.400
signaling an African drive to reclaim its cultural&nbsp;
identity after centuries of European pillaging.&nbsp;

00:39:03.120 --> 00:39:07.760
Efforts to protect sea turtles in Tunisia&nbsp;
are working, says new scientific research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.760 --> 00:39:14.480
that shows nests on Kuriat Island have increased&nbsp;
from 11 to more than 40 since 1997. Turtles are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:19.360
keystone species, meaning their disappearance&nbsp;
would disproportionately damage ecosystems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:19.360 --> 00:39:23.280
and their increased presence in this&nbsp;
area—despite threats from people, climate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.280 --> 00:39:28.080
and pollution—is good news for the reptiles,&nbsp;
and the workers striving to save them.&nbsp;

00:39:28.720 --> 00:39:32.400
Two Chinese bidders, with the&nbsp;
pseudonyms Super Duper and Hitman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.400 --> 00:39:38.880
bought a Belgian racing pigeon for a&nbsp;
record price of $1.9 million. Belgium is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.880 --> 00:39:43.920
the world’s leading racing pigeon breeding&nbsp;
country and, thanks to a booming economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:43.920 --> 00:39:49.600
the Chinese upper class have a lot of money&nbsp;
to spend even compared to a decade ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:49.600 --> 00:39:54.720
So why not invest in something like a pigeon? The&nbsp;
bird will likely be bred with another genetically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:59.600
superior pigeon to create a super-bird that&nbsp;
will dominate in racing circles, and betting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.600 --> 00:40:04.160
on those is just one area where the Chinese are&nbsp;
spending significant amounts of their yuan.&nbsp;

00:40:04.160 --> 00:40:09.680
Eleven months in Argentina sounds nice, but&nbsp;
for former Bolivian president Evo Morales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:09.680 --> 00:40:13.360
it was more of an exile. He&nbsp;
reentered Bolivia this November,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:13.360 --> 00:40:18.800
after a 2019 campaign for a fourth presidential&nbsp;
term became marred by fraud allegations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.800 --> 00:40:23.440
leading him to flee the country. A caretaker&nbsp;
government has managed affairs since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:28.080
but when Morales’ former economic minister&nbsp;
recently assumed leadership, it paved the way for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:28.080 --> 00:40:33.360
Morales to return—though it’s unclear what role&nbsp;
he’ll play in the country’s new reality. If it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.360 --> 00:40:38.800
sounds turbulent, it is. Latin American countries&nbsp;
continue to struggle with government instability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.800 --> 00:40:44.640
and this is just one more on the list.&nbsp;
 The financial situation in Cuba worsened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.640 --> 00:40:48.640
this year when the United States placed&nbsp;
increased restrictions on the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:48.640 --> 00:40:53.440
forcing the closure of 400 Western Union&nbsp;
offices and potentially cutting off a stream of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:53.440 --> 00:40:58.640
remittances from abroad. The United States claims&nbsp;
the move came in response to the Cuban government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:58.640 --> 00:41:03.440
purportedly siphoning a cut of the incoming&nbsp;
money, but officials there say the move was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:03.440 --> 00:41:08.480
politically motivated. Cuban-American relations&nbsp;
have become more tenuous under Trump, but the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:08.480 --> 00:41:13.040
Biden administration is expected to reinstate&nbsp;
diplomatic relations, especially since Cuban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:13.040 --> 00:41:18.000
refugee Alejandro Mayorkas has been nominated&nbsp;
to lead the Department of Homeland Security.&nbsp;

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:23.440
Hopping one island over, Haiti’s local president&nbsp;
of FIFA—the governing body for soccer—has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:28.560
shut out, and not in the winning way. Haitian&nbsp;
Football President Yves Jean-Bart was issued a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:28.560 --> 00:41:33.600
lifetime ban from all football-related activities&nbsp;
after he was found guilty of sexually harassing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:40.640
and abusing female players, including minors.&nbsp;
He was also fined roughly $1.1 million. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:40.640 --> 00:41:45.600
are lauding the punishment, saying abuse should&nbsp;
be rooted out of any industry, at any level.&nbsp;

00:41:46.320 --> 00:41:52.080
South Sudan is the world’s youngest country,&nbsp;
formed in 2011. Since then, it’s experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:58.480
almost a decade of civil war but earlier this year&nbsp;
feuding parties finally signed a unity agreement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:58.480 --> 00:42:02.000
Millions of people continue to flee the&nbsp;
country and suffer from extreme hunger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:07.280
but the establishment of a ruling party offers&nbsp;
one step in the right direction toward stability.&nbsp;

00:42:07.920 --> 00:42:13.760
President Pierre Nkurunziza of Burundi died&nbsp;
suddenly of a heart attack at the age of 55.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:13.760 --> 00:42:18.560
He leaves behind a legacy as an autocratic ruler&nbsp;
known for committing a variety of human rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:18.560 --> 00:42:23.760
abuses, and his death in June came just weeks&nbsp;
after the country had already elected a successor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:29.040
which only happened because Nkurunziza did not&nbsp;
seek reelection. His death will have impacts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:29.040 --> 00:42:34.480
on regional and global relationships, potentially&nbsp;
allowing for increased unity under new leadership.

00:42:35.200 --> 00:42:39.920
Despite the pandemic, the Dominican Republic&nbsp;
has hosted more than two dozen feature film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:39.920 --> 00:42:44.720
and TV production shoots this year, which&nbsp;
poured $80 million into the local economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.720 --> 00:42:50.560
smashing a previous annual record of $30&nbsp;
million in 2017. Producers track their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:50.560 --> 00:42:55.760
local expenditures because part of the country’s&nbsp;
international incentive for filming there is a 25%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:55.760 --> 00:43:00.400
rebate through a transferable tax credit.&nbsp;
That’s attractive to American filmmakers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:04.880
like M. Night Shyamalan who took advantage of&nbsp;
it this year, because they can spend 25% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:04.880 --> 00:43:09.680
wages in the Dominican Republic and maintain&nbsp;
tax incentive status in the United States.&nbsp;

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:14.400
A deer stealing their rifle isn’t something&nbsp;
that hunters would think they’d have to worry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:14.400 --> 00:43:18.720
about when out stalking, but that’s what&nbsp;
happened to one in the Czech Republic when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.720 --> 00:43:23.520
his dog startled the deer, which ran toward&nbsp;
him and looped his rifle with an antler.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:28.000
The deer was seen a mile away, with&nbsp;
the gun still in tow. Reportedly it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:32.880
wasn’t loaded, but had it been, a deer claiming&nbsp;
self-defense might have worked in this case.&nbsp;

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:38.400
Also filed under bizarre sightings is a tiny&nbsp;
child floating on an inflatable unicorn in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:38.400 --> 00:43:44.000
Meditteranean Sea. The 3-year-old girl was swept&nbsp;
into the water while playing on a beach in Greece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:49.120
but rescued shortly after by a passing ferryboat.&nbsp;
She was delivered back to her family safely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.120 --> 00:43:51.280
and, yes, the unicorn made it too.&nbsp;

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:57.840
When Brazilian Maya Gabeira surfed a 73.5-foot&nbsp;
or 22.4 meter wave off the coast of Portugal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:57.840 --> 00:44:03.360
in February, she didn’t just ride the biggest wave&nbsp;
ever for a woman—it was the largest wave surfed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:03.360 --> 00:44:10.080
anyone, period, during the 2019-2020 season. She&nbsp;
is the first woman in professional surfing to top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:14.880
all men in terms of wave size in a season, but&nbsp;
with another female surfer earning spot number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:14.880 --> 00:44:19.680
two on the big-wave list this year, it’s&nbsp;
clear she may not hold the title for long.&nbsp;

00:44:20.240 --> 00:44:24.640
The title for the highest smoking rate in&nbsp;
the world goes to Jordan, where a shocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:24.640 --> 00:44:29.920
80% of the male population says they use&nbsp;
smoke or use nicotine products daily. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:29.920 --> 00:44:35.600
was a status previously held by Indonesia, and&nbsp;
bucks the global trend of declining tobacco use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:35.600 --> 00:44:40.480
Multinational tobacco companies are still allowed&nbsp;
to hold a large amount of influence in Jordan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:40.480 --> 00:44:44.240
from funding school programs to being&nbsp;
featured on the prime minister’s social media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.240 --> 00:44:48.000
and this widespread interference&nbsp;
is likely what’s increasing use.&nbsp;

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:53.840
Rapper Cardi B may have overstepped her&nbsp;
political boundaries when she posted an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:53.840 --> 00:44:59.760
advertisement supporting Armenia to her 76&nbsp;
million Instagram followers. Like all wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:59.760 --> 00:45:03.440
the conflict between Azerbaijan&nbsp;
and Armenia has been a tragic one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:03.440 --> 00:45:07.600
that ended with Armenia’s defeat and a pact&nbsp;
between the two countries over the disputed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:07.600 --> 00:45:13.280
territory of Nagorno-Karabakh, which will remain&nbsp;
with Azerbaijan. But Cardi B was only trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:13.280 --> 00:45:18.560
help a real estate agent friend when she made the&nbsp;
post, and has since apologized amid the backlash.&nbsp;

00:45:19.280 --> 00:45:24.320
Unstaffed grocery stores accessed by logging&nbsp;
in with BankID, the national identification app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:24.320 --> 00:45:29.200
operated by Sweden’s banks, seems like something&nbsp;
only Sweden could pull off in remote areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:29.200 --> 00:45:34.160
which is why they’re probably the ones doing&nbsp;
it. Amid a drastic decline in food markets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:34.160 --> 00:45:39.360
across the country, mostly in rural areas, the&nbsp;
company Livfs has installed almost 20 small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:39.360 --> 00:45:43.760
container stores that are fully stocked&nbsp;
with meat, vegetables, dairy, and desserts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:48.640
Customers simply swipe an app to enter and&nbsp;
pay for groceries, no store clerk needed.&nbsp;

00:45:49.280 --> 00:45:53.040
Israeli flights traveling to and from the&nbsp;
United Arab Emirates can now cross through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:53.040 --> 00:45:57.840
Saudi Arabian airspace, marking the first time&nbsp;
that commercial jets on this route can pass over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:57.840 --> 00:46:02.160
the kingdom’s territory. The announcement cuts&nbsp;
travel time between the two countries in half,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:02.160 --> 00:46:08.240
to three and a half hours, and signals normalizing&nbsp;
relations, both diplomatically and economically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:08.240 --> 00:46:14.000
According to reports, the decision to open the&nbsp;
airspace came at the request of the UAE. It became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:14.000 --> 00:46:18.880
the third Arab country to acknowledge relations&nbsp;
with Israel, behind Egypt and Jordan, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.880 --> 00:46:23.760
development signals some progress in an otherwise&nbsp;
unstable region—though Saudi Arabia was quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:23.760 --> 00:46:29.040
to note that opening airspace didn’t change its&nbsp;
country’s stance on the Israel-Palestine issue.

00:46:29.680 --> 00:46:34.080
Despite having previously faced murder&nbsp;
charges, Juan Carlos Bonilla Valladares,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:34.080 --> 00:46:39.680
aka “The Tiger,” was still named police chief&nbsp;
in 2012 in Honduras. So it may not come as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:39.680 --> 00:46:44.880
surprise that he didn’t uphold the tenets of the&nbsp;
job, and is now charged with conspiring to smuggle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:44.880 --> 00:46:50.400
cocaine into the United States… but the plot&nbsp;
thickens. He wasn’t smuggling cocaine for just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:54.160
anyone, but doing the dirty work for&nbsp;
the Honduran president and his brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:54.160 --> 00:46:58.080
the latter of which was convicted for&nbsp;
drug trafficking in New York last year.&nbsp;

00:46:58.720 --> 00:47:03.520
In Hungary, a scientist accidentally&nbsp;
created a new fish hybrid in a lab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:03.520 --> 00:47:08.800
dubbed the sturddlefish. The combination of&nbsp;
Russian sturgeon, whose eggs make premium caviar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:08.800 --> 00:47:13.120
and American paddlefish, a filter feeder&nbsp;
found in less than half of the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:13.120 --> 00:47:17.600
is an unlikely one that would probably never&nbsp;
have occurred without human intervention—based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:17.600 --> 00:47:22.720
on geography alone. And while there’s no real&nbsp;
practical purpose to the awkward-looking creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:22.720 --> 00:47:28.080
it does demonstrate that species’ genetics are&nbsp;
sometimes more similar than previously thought.&nbsp;

00:47:28.080 --> 00:47:33.440
Also in the neighborhood is the proposed&nbsp;
Baltic-Black Sea waterway, a 1,240-mile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:33.440 --> 00:47:38.080
2,000 kilometer inland shipping route that would&nbsp;
cut through Poland, Ukraine, and some of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:38.080 --> 00:47:42.720
ecologically rich parts of Belarus. Oh, and&nbsp;
it would involve dredging up land inside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:42.720 --> 00:47:48.080
Chernobyl exclusion zone, which could potentially&nbsp;
expose millions of people to latent nuclear waste.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:48.080 --> 00:47:52.400
Proponents say it would spur an economic boon&nbsp;
for the region, while opponents argue it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:52.400 --> 00:47:57.680
destroy the “Amazon of Europe.” It may end up&nbsp;
happening piecemeal, with Ukraine and Belarus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:57.680 --> 00:48:02.640
committing—and already starting in some cases—to&nbsp;
dredging up parts of their tributary rivers.

00:48:03.280 --> 00:48:06.000
Tajikistan is one of the poorest countries in east&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:06.000 --> 00:48:11.280
Asia and many residents cross into Russia for&nbsp;
higher-paying jobs and higher education. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:11.280 --> 00:48:15.920
Russian-speaking population in this country&nbsp;
has dwindled 10-fold in four decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:15.920 --> 00:48:20.000
but there’s new interest in teaching children&nbsp;
their neighbor’s language, marked by officials’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:24.320
announcement that they would build five more&nbsp;
Russian-speaking schools—with a capacity for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:24.320 --> 00:48:30.640
1,200 students in each—in addition to the 10 that&nbsp;
have been constructed in just the past two years.&nbsp;

00:48:30.640 --> 00:48:34.320
A musician in Austria is pulling&nbsp;
a different sort of string:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:34.320 --> 00:48:40.720
that of Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart’s 250-year-old&nbsp;
violin. The musician took a leap when he asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:40.720 --> 00:48:45.680
the foundation that owns the violin if he could&nbsp;
borrow it to play and record the violin concertos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:45.680 --> 00:48:49.360
on the exact instrument Mozart&nbsp;
composed them on between 1773&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:49.360 --> 00:48:55.840
and 1775. The answer was yes, and he performed&nbsp;
them in October to small, live audiences.&nbsp;

00:48:56.560 --> 00:49:00.320
A group of United Nations officials&nbsp;
signed a letter to four countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:00.320 --> 00:49:03.680
protesting the development of a&nbsp;
massive mine in Papua New Guinea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:03.680 --> 00:49:08.720
citing grave concerns both for the environment&nbsp;
and human rights. If built, the mine would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:08.720 --> 00:49:13.920
the largest in the nation’s history, and&nbsp;
potentially produce $1.5 billion of gold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:13.920 --> 00:49:18.880
copper, and silver annually for 30 years.&nbsp;
But that money would mostly go to&nbsp; Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:18.880 --> 00:49:22.720
investors in the Australian-registered mining&nbsp;
company, and the real costs could come in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.720 --> 00:49:27.200
form of a catastrophic environmental disaster&nbsp;
on the Sepik River, where it would be located.&nbsp;

00:49:28.000 --> 00:49:32.880
More than 43 million Tweets amplifying&nbsp;
positive news coverage of Serbian President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:36.080
Aleksandar Vučić were linked to&nbsp;
his Serbian Progressive party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:36.080 --> 00:49:41.120
and Twitter subsequently blocked 8,500 accounts&nbsp;
for attempts to undermine public conversation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:41.120 --> 00:49:44.640
The tweets were making his governing&nbsp;
look better than it actually was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:44.640 --> 00:49:48.560
and this isn’t just happening in Serbia.&nbsp;
The tech company blocked some additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:49.280 --> 00:49:54.240
13,000 accounts in countries like Egypt, Honduras,&nbsp;
and Saudi Arabia for doing the same thing.&nbsp;

00:49:55.600 --> 00:50:02.320
Serbia is the 98th largest country in the&nbsp;
world, meaning we’re halfway to 195. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:02.320 --> 00:50:07.040
